      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that, that makes much more sense. so it's basically a pressure-sensing mechanism. i also read somewhere that besides renin, there are other substances or \"hidden\" things like bradykinin that get involved in this whole mess, making the system even more unpredictable. can you explain how that works? like, how does the reduction in angiotensin II impact those other pathways, and does that add to the complexity of how the body tries to compensate?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! The RAAS system is indeed complex and involves multiple feedback loops and interactions with other pathways. One of these \"hidden\" substances is bradykinin, a peptide that causes blood vessels to dilate, leading to a decrease in blood pressure. Under normal circumstances, ACE also breaks down bradykinin, so when ACE inhibitors are used, bradykinin levels increase.\n\nThe increase in bradykinin can contribute to the side effects of ACE inhibitors, such as a persistent dry cough, because bradykinin can stimulate sensory nerves in the respiratory tract. However, this increase in bradykinin also has a beneficial effect on the kidneys. Bradykinin can help to dilate the afferent arterioles of the glomeruli, which can increase the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) and help to protect the kidneys from the effects of hypertension.\n\nIn addition to bradykinin, there are other substances and pathways that can be affected by the reduction in angiotensin II. For example, angiotensin II normally inhibits the secretion of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) and brain natriuretic peptide (BNP), which are hormones that help to reduce blood volume",
